7
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:35,120
We learned all about acids and bases in the general chemistry series, and we also learned

8
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:38,840
about the concept of an acid-base titration.

9
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:44,760
This is a neutralization reaction, where we use an acid or base of known concentration to

10
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:47,960
determine the concentration of the other reagent.

11
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:53,040
This is because we can find the stoichiometric amount of acid or base titrant that must be

12
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:59,780
used to precisely neutralize the analyte, and then use the volume of titrant added to calculate

13
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:02,200
the concentration of the analyte.

14
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:07,620
For example, one mole of hydrochloric acid reacts with one mole of sodium hydroxide to

15
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:11,640
produce one mole of sodium chloride and one mole of water.

16
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:15,960
Knowing the number of moles we add of one substance tells us something about the number

17
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:19,400
of moles of the other substance initially present.

18
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:24,560
The point at which the number of moles of acid and base precisely neutralize one another

19
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:27,260
is called the equivalence point.

20
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:31,920
To get to this point, you have to slowly add your acid or base little by little until

21
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:36,160
the reagents are exhausted, thus leaving only water and salt.

22
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:41,640
We have already gone over titration calculations in lecture-based tutorials, so that will not

23
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:46,760
be the focus here, although we will discuss these calculations in a general way.

24
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:50,880
Rather than actually performing stoichiometric calculations, we are going to focus on the

25
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:56,400
details of physically performing the titration, which should help make any relevant calculations

26
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:57,800
easier to do.

27
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:01,960
For example, we have already learned that to get to the equivalence point of a titration

28
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:08,880
between a strong acid and base, like HCl and NaOH, we can measure the pH until we

29
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:14,960
reach a pH of 7, since precisely equimolar amounts of strong acid and strong base will

30
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:19,340
leave only salt and water, which gives us neutral pH.

31
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:24,960
But rather than measuring pH, what we normally use in chemistry labs when performing titrations

32
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:26,400
is an indicator.

33
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:31,960
An indicator is a substance that exhibits a visible color change once the titration reaches

34
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:33,720
its equivalence point.

35
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:38,820
For many acid-based titrations, a common indicator will be phenolphthalein.

36
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:43,760
This is because it turns bright pink as the solution is changing from acidic to basic,

37
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:49,580
as upon having just neutralized all the acid, any additional base will react with phenolphthalein

38
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:54,680
instead, and it is the deprotonated form that glows pink, so as soon as we see this

39
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:59,520
color persist in solution, we'll know that we're at the equivalence point.

40
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:03,540
So to summarize, we start with an acid, we can add a bit of the indicator, and

41
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:05,380
then titrate with a base.

42
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:10,620
Again, the solution will stay clear until it suddenly turns pink at the equivalence point,

43
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:15,980
notifying us that the titration is complete, and allowing us to do our calculations.

44
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:21,020
Today we will be using titration to find the concentration of a hydrochloric acid solution

45
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:25,800
by using 0.1 molar sodium hydroxide as the titrant.

46
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:32,100
To start, we'll need a ring stand fixed with a burette clamp, an Erlenmeyer flask,

47
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:41,100
a burette, a beaker, a glass funnel, our acid and our base, some indicator, and a graduated

48
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:42,100
cylinder.

49
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:45,940
First, let's assemble our titration setup.

50
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:50,140
We'll add our clean burette to the ring stand and clamp.

51
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:54,260
This is where we'll add our base, which again is referred to as the titrant in this

52
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:55,340
case.

53
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:59,300
Our acid will go in an Erlenmeyer flask below the burette.

54
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:01,540
This is our analyte.

55
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:05,540
Before adding base to the burette, let's make sure it works correctly.

56
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:08,380
We can add water on top using a funnel.

57
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:12,780
The burette can be tall, so you might need to lower it or step up on something.

58
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:16,500
Sometimes the stopcock leaks, and you don't want your base to leak out, as that will

59
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:20,300
affect your calculation, so that's why we start with water.

60
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:24,260
After you see that there are no leaks, turn the stopcock parallel to the burette

61
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:27,820
to open it, making sure it is not clogged.

62
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:33,300
After you ensure that water flows well, remove all the water and close the stopcock.

63
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:37,960
Next we're going to load up our base, making sure that we are wearing safety goggles and

64
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:40,660
gloves as we are using corrosive chemicals.

65
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:45,640
Let's fill the burette all the way to the top using a funnel.

66
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:50,620
In your lab notebook, note the starting volume reading on the burette.

67
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:54,220
Remember to read from the bottom of the meniscus and record it to the appropriate

68
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:56,740
number of significant figures.

69
00:04:56,740 --> 00:05:01,180
Now let's put our acid in the flask, and place this directly below the burette.

70
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:06,540
In this experiment we are using 20 milliliters of HCl solution, but again we don't know

71
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:07,860
the concentration.

72
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:10,940
Let's also add a few drops of the indicator.

73
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:13,820
We're now ready for the titration.

74
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:18,740
We will start with what we call a rough titration, or quick and dirty titration.

75
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:23,340
This is a quick run to get an idea of where our equivalence point will be.

76
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:27,580
This rough titration saves a lot of time, but it is not very accurate, so we won't

77
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:29,820
use this data in our results.

78
00:05:29,820 --> 00:05:34,020
To do this, let's open the burette and let the base go down quickly as you swirl

79
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:38,380
the flask, which could also be stirred with a magnetic stir bar if you desire.

80
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:43,100
You'll stop every now and then to check if there is a color change that's persistent.

81
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:46,660
When you see this persistent change, you will know that you have reached the equivalence

82
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:48,020
point.

83
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:52,260
Record the end volume reading on the burette, and subtract that from the initial reading

84
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:54,120
to get the volume added.

85
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:58,640
This volume is an estimate of how much it will take to reach the equivalence point.

86
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:03,740
But because we poured very quickly, we have most likely added a bit more than was needed.

87
00:06:03,740 --> 00:06:06,260
The next titration will be more accurate.

88
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:10,540
Since we have an idea of how much base we need, we can quickly add about three quarters

89
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:13,480
of the volume we recorded from the rough titration.

90
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:16,700
We know that we won't get to the equivalence point with this amount.

91
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:18,920
But after that, we have to be more careful.

92
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:24,200
A safe approach is to add a half a milliliter at a time, but you can go as slowly as one

93
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:25,960
drop at a time.

94
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:30,440
After you add half a milliliter, you'll swirl and see if there is a color change.

95
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:34,800
When the base drops into the acid, you may see some fleeting color change right when

96
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:39,200
the drop enters, but it doesn't stay like that when you mix the solution, which means

97
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:42,000
the equivalence point has not been reached.

98
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,760
We need a color change that remains even after mixing.

99
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:48,400
Keep adding, mixing, and checking.

100
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:52,240
It is important to be careful when we're close, as we don't want to miss the equivalence

101
00:06:52,240 --> 00:06:54,460
point by adding too much.

102
00:06:54,460 --> 00:07:00,440
As you can see, this pink color is not changing anymore, so we are at the equivalence point.

103
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:05,280
Record the final volume reading on the burette, and get the difference from the first reading.

104
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:10,780
As we already know, the volume of base added, multiplied by its concentration, over the

105
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:15,180
volume of acid will give us the concentration of our acid.

106
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:20,640
In this type of experiment, we generally perform three titrations for our concentration determination

107
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:22,140
to be more accurate.

108
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:26,300
The average of the three numbers will give us a solid estimate for the concentration of

109
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:28,460
the acid analyte.

110
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:33,300
Titration certainly gets much more complicated than this, even simply by utilizing a weak

111
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:38,760
acid or base as either the titrant or analyte, which will change the pH of the equivalence

112
00:07:38,760 --> 00:07:41,860
point and therefore the indicator that is used.

113
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:46,980
There are also several other types of titrations, such as redox titrations.

114
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:51,860
But for now, we have covered the most basic titration setup, so that we have a firm grasp

115
00:07:51,860 --> 00:08:06,590
of how to do this type of analytical chemistry.
